WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.870
A little piece of the UK in the heart of Berlin's
Kreuzberg district: 'Broken English' has sold

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:08.690
groceries and souvenirs from the island nation
for 24 years.

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:09.690
"This is my final purchase here at 'Broken
English', as this shop will soon close.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.969
There are also Brexit victims in Berlin."

00:00:11.969 --> 00:00:16.610
Dale Carr, a native Briton, has spent almost
half her life in this shop.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:21.410
Now, she's retiring -- but cannot find anyone
who wants to take over the shop.

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:25.779
In this era of Brexit, trying to make a living
selling imported goods from Britain seems

00:00:25.779 --> 00:00:27.000
just too risky.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.820
"I am not sure who would take the chance at
the moment.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.559
To find himself in a position, where they
are not able to get hold of the stock that's

00:00:34.559 --> 00:00:35.559
required.

00:00:35.559 --> 00:00:40.360
There is a possibility of going through Ireland,
that we thought of ourselves, but of course

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.960
Ireland has its own issues at the time.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.399
It's just simply not knowing."

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:50.980
Ever since the Brexit referendum two-and-a-half
years ago, Britain's leaving the EU has been

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:54.590
a constant topic of conversation here in Broken
English.

00:00:54.590 --> 00:01:00.079
In order to remain EU citizens, many of Dale
Carr's customers have taken on German citizenship

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:02.520
– including the shopkeeper herself.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.080
What is your view on the Brexit?

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.210
Dale Carr: "Oh dear...well.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:13.170
My own personal view, that it is a terrible
mistake.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.640
That the Uk is making.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:22.040
I simply don´t understand the politics behind
it or what they possibly think what will be

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.740
better."

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.400
Brexit is to blame for her now giving up.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.490
A clearance sale began this week in Broken
English.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:30.760
"Bourbon biscuits, John Smith beer, a fancy
gin, baked beans, or Marmite: love 'em or

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:31.950
hate 'em, these goods will no longer be so
easy to find in Berlin.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:32.950
But this also has to do with a piece of Britons'/British
identity in Berlin."

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:33.950
"This is like home.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.210
You walk in look at the environment here.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:39.049
It's very nice it's very quaint it's really
England here."

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:42.460
"I think it's a shame I think it's really
sad.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:48.770
But I guess you can't really help the effects
of this uncertainty makes it really hard for

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.570
businesses to carry on.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:52.710
"Horrible.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.290
I need the tea from here.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:56.030
I need the sweets from here.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:02:01.520
And whenever one of my English friends has
a birthday, I buy presents here."

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.939
The greatest present for Dale Carr would be
an 'exit from the Brexit'.

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:11.280
As unlikely as that may be, she still holds
out hope.

